WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.972
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.560
TOM FEINER: We've
all been there.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.970
You're on call, in charge
of an entire production

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.510
system such as this.

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:26.080
It's peak traffic
on the East Coast,

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.680
but you're on the other
side of the world,

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.190
sleeping like a baby.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:33.030
You have no idea what's
about to hit you.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.755
In my nightmares,
here's what I see.

00:00:38.755 --> 00:00:43.430
The pager goes off saying
something like this.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:45.110
You wipe the sleep
from your eyes,

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:46.730
grab a computer to investigate.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:48.260
You're still half
asleep and have

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:51.590
no idea what's really going on.

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:54.440
Traffic jams are forming
all around the area.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:58.820
Users have no idea where
to go to avoid traffic.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:00.710
Time to start debugging.

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.590
Millions of people
are counting on you.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.260
You open the CPU graph
for this server group,

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:13.400
as you know the
service is CPU bound.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.740
And it looks normal.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.360
All the scaling did its thing.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.360
It was set to keep
the CPU under 45%.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.700
And that's exactly what it did.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.050
The number of instances doubled
when the CPU started to climb.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:29.400
So why isn't the
service working?

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.100
You feel a cold sweat forming.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.920
This is not going to end well.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.810
All instances in
this server group

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:40.720
are currently completely
failing their health checks.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.350
You connect to a
sample server, and you

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:44.990
see that it's at 100% CPU.

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:47.440
Why?

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.205
Pick a jstack, and nothing comes
to you, can't see anything.

00:01:51.205 --> 00:01:54.034
So you restart the
application, look at the logs,

00:01:54.034 --> 00:01:55.450
just to see the
start-up sequence,

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:57.920
see if you can find anything.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:58.970
But it all looks fine.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:00.590
The dependencies are loading.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:02.480
The application starts fine.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:09.030
But yet, once it's started,
CPU jumps right back to 100%.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:14.110
A bad feeling creeps up
your spine at this point.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:17.920
You connect to another server,
and this one's completely idle.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:19.780
How come?

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.280
You look around,
and you can't even

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.560
find the application
running there at all.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.930
What's going on here?

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:29.760
Was the CPU graph from
earlier lying to me?

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.530
CPU graphs usually don't lie.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:34.900
You check it again,
and you realize,

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.030
it's an average CPU graph.

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:39.140
How did I miss that?

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:43.090
So it appears, that some
servers are running at 100% CPU,

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:46.990
burning up, practically
thrashing, not doing anything,

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:50.770
while others are
completely idle.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:54.040
So you ask yourself,
am I in over my head?

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:01.740
Your heart tries to pound
its way out of your chest

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:06.030
when you realize you have
no idea how to fix this.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:10.260
Then, while looking at the
logs, suddenly, instance

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.170
gets terminated, thrown out.

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:14.470
And you can't debug anymore.

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:16.720
In the UI, you see
even more instances

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.690
committing suicide,
instances which were just

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:22.420
started a few minutes ago.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.070
This doesn't make any sense.

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:29.090
The system's scaled up, only
to kill itself in the loop?

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:31.460
Come on.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:35.360
Is it time to admit failure?

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.842
Wake up some other people
from the team to help?

00:03:37.842 --> 00:03:39.800
You're supposed to know
what you're doing here.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.040
You're a professional.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.380
You think for a minute.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:46.540
What on Earth could
be causing this?

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:52.230
And nothing comes to mind.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:53.110
OK, OK.

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:56.100
So you pick up the phone,
about to call for help.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.810
You're about to hit Send.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.340
Then it hits you.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.180
That's auto-healing.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.350
Auto-healing is like
the immune system,

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:05.890
it's supposed to replace
unhealthy instances.

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.150
But in this case,
it's all of them,

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:11.300
so it's doing much
more harm than good.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:12.730
You dig through
the console to try

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:16.720
to find out where to
shut down auto-healing,

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.399
wasting a few more
precious minutes.

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:25.110
You find another server
which is still up and idle.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:29.911
Why is the application
not running there?

00:04:29.911 --> 00:04:33.340
You keep combing the
logs, trying to find--

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:34.810
you're looking
everywhere you can,

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:38.990
till you finally find this.

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:40.970
Apparently, each
server, upon startup,

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.340
connects to a
configuration server

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:44.870
to download this configuration.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.920
Without it, it's
pretty much useless.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.260
So this configuration
server, apparently

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:54.170
fell over and managed to do
two things at the same time.

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:57.310
One, it's taking down this
entire production system

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:02.120
with it, turning a perfectly
nice auto-scaling group

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:05.760
into a set of zombie servers
which are committing suicide.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.550
And two, is to give
you a heart attack.

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:12.700
Now that you
understand the problem,

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:16.590
you quickly fix the
configuration server,

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:23.580
scale up some more servers,
and we're back to normal [SIGH]

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:25.300
finally.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:30.560
You double check, just to
make sure, and go back to bed,

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.990
patting yourself on the
back for saving the day.

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:38.470
You try to sleep, but
find it impossible.

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:41.240
Something's still bothering you.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.770
Did we really save the day?

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.070
We were down for 20 minutes.

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:50.650
20 minutes with millions
of users frustrated.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:55.720
Then you realize, your
job is not to be a ninja,

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:57.760
even though it's fun sometimes.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:05:59.530
Your job is to
prevent these kind

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:02.760
of failures in the first place.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:07.480
You remember hearing once about
the immutable server pattern,

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:09.980
and a light pops
up in your head.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:13.450
If this fleet of servers
was immutable and completely

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:18.900
self-contained, all of this
would have been avoided.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:21.500
Baking eliminates the
external dependencies

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:23.990
such as this
configuration server,

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:27.140
improving the stability of
the system, not to mention

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:29.270
faster boot times.

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:31.970
So you wake up in the
morning and run off

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:34.820
to work to start baking
immutable images.

00:06:40.447 --> 00:06:42.395
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:48.820
STEVEN KIM: There is an
astronaut saying in space

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:54.150
"there is no problem so bad
that you can't make it worse."

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:59.280
And it speaks to dealing
with a level of complexity

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:03.180
and pressure like in this story,
where you can quickly feel

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:06.180
like you're in sinking sand.

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:10.200
The complexity of rolling out
software continues to increase.

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:12.900
New architectures,
micro-services architecture,

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.180
the scope of deployment,
multi-regional deployments,

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:17.470
global deployments.

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:20.670
And, as a response,
what we've done,

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:27.070
is we've introduced measures
and processes that slow us down.

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:30.580
And I think, at this
point, most of us

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:34.780
don't think that rolling
out the production

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:41.830
hourly, daily, weekly, monthly,
quarterly is a good idea,

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:44.400
let alone feasible.

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:45.900
And meanwhile,
what's on the table,

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:51.450
is early and often feedback that
we're doing the right things,

00:07:51.450 --> 00:07:54.630
frequent opportunities
to pivot and change

00:07:54.630 --> 00:08:00.150
direction when we need to, and
some side effects of doing more

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:03.171
frequent roll-outs, such
as the scope of change

00:08:03.171 --> 00:08:05.170
is smaller because you're
doing more frequently,

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:08.600
so there is
inherently less risk.

00:08:08.600 --> 00:08:11.800
So there's a lot
of talk out there

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:14.610
about continuous delivery.

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:17.590
But we need to talk
about really what's

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:20.110
important in terms
of first principles

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:23.610
behind continuous delivery
and not just the tooling.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:26.010
And Google's been
doing this a long time.

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:27.900
We have made our
share of mistakes.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.340
We have lessons learned.

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:31.925
And based on that, we
have a set of opinions.

00:08:34.362 --> 00:08:36.320
So we want to go to share
those lessons learned

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:40.179
with you, along with
some tangible examples.

00:08:40.179 --> 00:08:46.190
Continuous delivery can't just
be about better orchestration.

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:49.710
It's one of the things,
but that's not it.

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:52.614
So my hope throughout
this talk--

00:08:52.614 --> 00:08:54.030
all of you guys
came and attended,

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:56.460
because you care about this--

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.000
my hope is to get
us, the community,

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.690
thinking about, and talking
about, and implementing

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:07.170
the right first
principles as we go

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:10.810
to take on continuous delivery.

00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:11.980
My name is Steven Kim.

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:15.310
I'm an engineering manager
out of New York City.

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:16.360
And this is Tom Feiner.

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:19.430
He's a systems operations
engineer for Waze,

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:21.370
if you didn't guess.

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:24.370
And we're here to talk to you
about continuous delivery,

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:26.084
and a tool called
the Spinnaker, which

00:09:26.084 --> 00:09:27.250
we want to walk you through.

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:36.570
Spinnaker is an open
source, multi cloud,

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:41.290
continuous delivery platform
developed at Netflix.

00:09:41.290 --> 00:09:43.980
Google joined the project
some three years ago,

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:49.170
and, along with other members
of the community like Kenzan,

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:55.440
we open sourced in
October, November of 2015.

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:58.850
Google and Netflix, we found a
kindred spirit in one another,

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:01.500
in the way we go to approach
the kinds of problems

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:03.810
that Tom described earlier.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:05.970
So what I want to do today is--

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:11.330
I want to share with you
four first principles.

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:14.400
And for each of these,
walk through how

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.280
they manifest at Waze
to ground them a bit

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:19.320
and give you some
tangible examples.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:20.897
And then at the
end, hopefully we'll

00:10:20.897 --> 00:10:22.980
have enough time we'll
walk through a simple demo.

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:29.860
The first principle is
immutable infrastructure

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:31.880
as Tom talked about.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:36.470
It means that once you deploy
something, you don't change it.

00:10:36.470 --> 00:10:41.380
When there's change, you
rebuild it up from the host up,

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:43.820
or the container up,
and then you tear down

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:45.350
the old one altogether.

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:48.620
How many of you guys have not
heard of immutable servers

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:52.260
or immutable infrastructures?

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:53.480
OK, great.

00:10:53.480 --> 00:10:55.517
So it's widely out there.

00:10:55.517 --> 00:10:56.600
Why do we want to do this?

00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:59.600
Why is immutable
infrastructure important?

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:04.070
We want our process of building,
and testing and deploying

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:07.620
and validating to be as
deterministic as possible.

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:09.620
We know that across
environments there

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:15.592
is a lack of permaticity where
there are configuration drifts.

00:11:15.592 --> 00:11:18.050
And, as you go and deploy and
promote move from environment

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:20.570
to environment,
that things changed.

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:23.814
And just because you know that
it worked in one environment--

00:11:23.814 --> 00:11:25.730
whether it be your
developer local workstation

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:28.919
or in a non-production staging
or stress environment--

00:11:28.919 --> 00:11:30.710
there's still a lot
out there through which

00:11:30.710 --> 00:11:32.960
things can go wrong
from that promotion

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:35.380
to the last production stage.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:37.660
So we want to go to
minimize changes slipping in

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:40.120
as we go to move up the runway.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:41.380
And here's the way it works.

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:44.230
In practice, you bake.

00:11:44.230 --> 00:11:45.730
So every time you
have a change, you

00:11:45.730 --> 00:11:52.299
bake an image using Packer
or, for those of you who

00:11:52.299 --> 00:11:54.590
are using configuration
management software like Puppet

00:11:54.590 --> 00:11:58.300
and Chef, you run that
as much as you can.

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.350
And then you bake
it into an image.

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:04.700
So in GCP, it's
Google GCE images.

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:07.160
And then, at deploy time, you
go ahead and dole those out

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:10.250
into GCE instances.

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:13.760
So for those of you who are
from the container world,

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:15.230
this seems really
familiar, right?

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:17.240
Because it's the same thing.

00:12:17.240 --> 00:12:18.930
You take your
application binaries,

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:20.220
you dependent runtime stacks.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:22.400
You find them in a
Dockerfile, package up you

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:25.760
configuration file into
a Docker image using

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:28.890
GCR build, which we
announced earlier last week,

00:12:28.890 --> 00:12:31.900
or your favorite service
such as Docker Hub or Quay.

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:35.710
And you go ahead and create
Docker container images.

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:37.460
And then you go ahead
and instantiate them

00:12:37.460 --> 00:12:44.070
using, for example, Kubernetes
scheduler into pods or whatnot.

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:46.650
So there's some other
benefits as well

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:48.611
to immutable infrastructure.

00:12:48.611 --> 00:12:50.360
If you remember, one
of the early messages

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:54.400
about the public
cloud was elasticity.

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:58.970
Elasticity had to do with
long-time window elasticity

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:02.380
that you don't have to go ahead
and buy upfront and then eat

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:05.510
the costs, but it also had to do
with responsiveness to demand--

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:08.240
spiky traffic, for example.

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:11.900
Well, for those of us who
might, for example, instantiate

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:15.110
an instance of VM, run your
Chef or Puppet scripts on it,

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:18.641
if that Puppet script takes 20
minutes or an hour and a half,

00:13:18.641 --> 00:13:20.390
you're not taking
advantage of elasticity.

00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:24.800
The demand has
come, and it's gone.

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:26.510
So there's some
other side effects

00:13:26.510 --> 00:13:30.020
like that where you have
faster start-up times.

00:13:30.020 --> 00:13:33.430
You can respond to demand much
quicker in traffic spikes.

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:35.810
And also, it opens
up opportunities

00:13:35.810 --> 00:13:39.440
to take advantage of things like
deployment strategies that are

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:41.330
very particular to the cloud.

00:13:41.330 --> 00:13:43.970
So immutable infrastructure
is an example at Waze

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:45.167
that Tom gave.

00:13:45.167 --> 00:13:47.750
It was somewhat rhetorical, but
I think you guys get the idea.

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:53.010
The second practice
is around the use

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:55.290
of deployment strategies.

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:57.720
There are a lot
of tools out there

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:00.160
that people can use to
go ahead and deploy out

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:02.370
the infrastructure and
deploy changes to code.

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:04.170
Jenkins is a popular one.

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:07.650
But I started by saying it can't
be just about orchestration.

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:09.899
And deployment strategies
is an example of this.

00:14:09.899 --> 00:14:11.940
So we're talking about
deployment strategies that

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:14.610
can specifically take
advantage of some

00:14:14.610 --> 00:14:15.900
of what you get in the cloud.

00:14:15.900 --> 00:14:17.896
So let's walk through a couple.

00:14:17.896 --> 00:14:20.970
Whoop, wrong button.

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:23.310
A simple first one
is called Red/black,

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:26.160
some people also
call it Blue/green.

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:31.140
And it's the notion that you
have a production cluster, V1,

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:34.410
and then you have a V1.1, and
you basically create a whole

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:36.687
new stack running that.

00:14:36.687 --> 00:14:38.520
And after it's been
validated through health

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:40.560
checks, for whatever
your criteria might be,

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:42.656
you cut over traffic.

00:14:42.656 --> 00:14:45.060
The advantage here is that
if something goes wrong,

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:48.180
you can quickly cut back.

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:50.580
There are some
disadvantages as, obviously,

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:52.980
2Xing the amount of
infrastructure and resources

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:54.780
required to go
ahead and do this.

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:57.240
But this is a simple example
where if something goes wrong,

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:58.948
you can go out and
very quickly cut back.

00:15:01.665 --> 00:15:03.040
An evolution of
this is something

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:05.000
that we're calling
rolling red/black.

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:08.330
And it's the notion that
you can go ahead and define

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:12.670
incrementality of traffic
percentage cut-overs

00:15:12.670 --> 00:15:14.790
with validation
gates in between.

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:20.224
And so we can simply say
1%, 5%, 25%, 50%, and 100%,

00:15:20.224 --> 00:15:22.890
and between each cut over to the
next percentage incrementality,

00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:24.860
you can go ahead and
set a validation gate.

00:15:24.860 --> 00:15:27.500
So it might be a
pipeline that you

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:29.780
run which might be very robust.

00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:35.140
Or it might be a simple smoke
test, or a functional probe.

00:15:35.140 --> 00:15:36.790
And it also has
the continuous take

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:38.530
on the advantage
of Red/black, which

00:15:38.530 --> 00:15:42.457
is if something ever goes wrong,
rolling back is immediate.

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:48.410
Another evolution of that
from there is Canary.

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:50.910
And Canary is a different
way to do the validation

00:15:50.910 --> 00:15:52.899
gates in between the
percentage roll-outs.

00:15:52.899 --> 00:15:54.690
But it takes a slightly
different approach.

00:15:54.690 --> 00:15:58.650
Rather than using functional
tests or probe or smoke tests,

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:00.379
we go to look at a
set of measurements

00:16:00.379 --> 00:16:01.920
which are much more
common regardless

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:03.450
of what your application is.

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:05.880
So we look at
underlying metrics.

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:09.120
So obviously things like
CPU and memory profiling,

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:13.657
but also latency, response
codes, error rates.

00:16:13.657 --> 00:16:15.240
And you take all
these, and you create

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:16.860
a weight by which
you go and say,

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:19.890
a certain Canary
score is acceptable.

00:16:19.890 --> 00:16:22.200
And that Canary score
also takes into effect

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:25.824
about an acceptable deviation
of that Canary score

00:16:25.824 --> 00:16:27.240
or the performance
characteristics

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:31.210
between your previous version
and your current version.

00:16:31.210 --> 00:16:33.060
So here's what it
means, test coverage

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:35.092
is always a cat-and-mouse game.

00:16:35.092 --> 00:16:37.240
Test coverage is at 80% today.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:40.150
Every CodeCommit will go to the
lower that has test coverage.

00:16:40.150 --> 00:16:41.550
It goes in that direction.

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:43.935
So with Canary, you can
actually go ahead and say, well,

00:16:43.935 --> 00:16:45.310
no matter what
we're rolling out,

00:16:45.310 --> 00:16:46.300
we're going to look
at a different set

00:16:46.300 --> 00:16:48.424
of characteristics that
are common to applications.

00:16:53.464 --> 00:16:55.630
So with that, let's go ahead
and ask Tom to go ahead

00:16:55.630 --> 00:16:57.490
and talk about what
that looks like at Waze.

00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:06.400
TOM FEINER: So 10
years ago, the cloud

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:09.880
did not exist, at least
not as it does today.

00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:13.060
Back then, most companies
ran their own infrastructure.

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:17.540
And, from what I've heard in
this conference, some still do.

00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:19.550
What's up with that?

00:17:19.550 --> 00:17:21.710
It wasn't pretty then,
and it isn't now.

00:17:24.430 --> 00:17:26.470
No matter how hard
you tried, you

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:29.550
could have sent API calls
to your hosting provider

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:32.630
10 years ago asking
for 500 servers,

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:36.580
and they would have just
dismissed you as some lunatic.

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:40.910
Luckily, these
days, it's possible.

00:17:40.910 --> 00:17:44.410
And, as I mentioned,
it's part of Spinnaker.

00:17:44.410 --> 00:17:49.300
So, using deployment
strategies, it's

00:17:49.300 --> 00:17:50.869
basically baked into Spinnaker.

00:17:50.869 --> 00:17:52.410
And we use that all
the time at Waze.

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:55.730
All right.

00:17:55.730 --> 00:17:57.140
This is how it looks like.

00:17:57.140 --> 00:17:59.560
Oh, I'm sorry.

00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:02.980
In a large system,
as Steven mentioned,

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:06.340
it's usually impossible to
test everything in advance.

00:18:06.340 --> 00:18:08.380
So we also use a Canary stage.

00:18:08.380 --> 00:18:11.170
It's not fancy like
the one Netflix uses,

00:18:11.170 --> 00:18:13.679
but it does the job.

00:18:13.679 --> 00:18:14.470
It looks like this.

00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:16.300
Basically, you have
a production system

00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:19.660
running with 100% of
traffic flowing to it.

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:22.240
You then run version
two in a Canary

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:26.510
and send a tiny bit
of traffic to it.

00:18:26.510 --> 00:18:29.910
You then run a Canary
analysis on each

00:18:29.910 --> 00:18:31.660
and every API of that server.

00:18:31.660 --> 00:18:35.267
So for each API, we test
for errors, latencies,

00:18:35.267 --> 00:18:36.225
and number of requests.

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:43.110
We perform a relative compare
against the production group.

00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:47.010
If it all works fine, we blow
that up to a production scale

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:50.230
and send 50% of traffic
to the new group,

00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:53.190
but still 50% of
traffic to the old one.

00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:55.710
We then run the
Canary analysis again,

00:18:55.710 --> 00:18:57.960
this time expecting a
very similar behavior

00:18:57.960 --> 00:18:59.340
between the two groups.

00:18:59.340 --> 00:19:02.280
If there's any
significant difference,

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:05.130
we can easily roll back by
just shifting traffic back

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:07.770
to the old group, and
debugging, and then destroying

00:19:07.770 --> 00:19:09.000
the old one.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:13.170
But if everything's fine, then
we just disable the old group

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:14.730
and eventually destroy it.

00:19:24.370 --> 00:19:32.320
STEVEN KIM: The third first
principle is automation.

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:36.010
So automation promotes
a consistency.

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:38.230
And a repeatability,
a consistency,

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:41.470
is important both in
success and failure modes.

00:19:41.470 --> 00:19:44.640
When things are failing, you
need a consistency of failure

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:46.330
to be able to do
root cause analysis.

00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:48.496
What really sucks is when
it fails in different ways

00:19:48.496 --> 00:19:50.020
every single time.

00:19:50.020 --> 00:19:53.550
And success, a
successful consistency

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:57.210
promotes maturity of process
that you can go and build

00:19:57.210 --> 00:19:59.250
on top of.

00:19:59.250 --> 00:20:02.610
And so it's about
identifying and putting

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:06.900
in mitigating measures,
without losing velocity.

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:10.140
So if you were, for example,
to go ahead and write--

00:20:10.140 --> 00:20:13.530
a number of tools out there that
does orchestration-- and say,

00:20:13.530 --> 00:20:17.070
step one, step two, do
this, and do a deploy.

00:20:17.070 --> 00:20:18.420
Something fails.

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:20.040
You go, oh, that
can't happen again.

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:21.210
I'm going to go ahead
and write a mediating

00:20:21.210 --> 00:20:22.834
measure so I can very
quickly roll back

00:20:22.834 --> 00:20:24.270
and, say, write something else.

00:20:24.270 --> 00:20:27.840
And the way that
this evolves, I said

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:31.800
at the beginning of the talk
that it slows process down,

00:20:31.800 --> 00:20:35.764
and it becomes very difficult
to go ahead and improve

00:20:35.764 --> 00:20:37.680
if you're slowing down
constantly, every time.

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:41.850
So it's difficult to improve
if you haven't instrumented.

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:45.780
It's difficult to-- or
less useful to instrument

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:48.330
if you haven't automated.

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:54.400
And it's hard to get to
a consistency and a scale

00:20:54.400 --> 00:20:57.490
of information that
gets interesting enough

00:20:57.490 --> 00:20:58.830
if you're not automating.

00:20:58.830 --> 00:21:02.890
So let me talk about what
I mean by automation.

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:08.260
So top level pipelines go out
and define and execute for you

00:21:08.260 --> 00:21:11.000
your process on how you go
ahead and release software.

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:14.610
So, for example, find an image
that you validated, looks good,

00:21:14.610 --> 00:21:16.750
go ahead and deploy
using Red/black strategy,

00:21:16.750 --> 00:21:19.090
do some rudimentary smoke
tests, wait a certain amount

00:21:19.090 --> 00:21:21.790
of period, go ahead and scale
one down and scale it up--

00:21:21.790 --> 00:21:23.470
all the different
deployment strategies

00:21:23.470 --> 00:21:25.190
that we talked about before.

00:21:25.190 --> 00:21:29.290
So this is good, but
the automation also

00:21:29.290 --> 00:21:33.460
has to happen in a more
fine, granular form

00:21:33.460 --> 00:21:34.780
underneath each of these steps.

00:21:34.780 --> 00:21:36.130
So let me show you.

00:21:36.130 --> 00:21:40.210
For that deploy to prod
Red/black [? 1 ?] stage,

00:21:40.210 --> 00:21:43.420
here's what Spinnaker does.

00:21:43.420 --> 00:21:46.150
For each stage, there
are a number of steps.

00:21:46.150 --> 00:21:48.660
And so it goes and
deploys in default.

00:21:48.660 --> 00:21:50.110
It shrinks the cluster.

00:21:50.110 --> 00:21:52.910
It disables it, seals it down.

00:21:52.910 --> 00:21:56.290
And then of each of those
steps, for any one given step,

00:21:56.290 --> 00:22:00.650
there are a number of tasks
that we go ahead and perform.

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:03.730
So this is where it gets
really tricky, determining

00:22:03.730 --> 00:22:04.749
the health of things.

00:22:04.749 --> 00:22:05.790
Do we need to go forward?

00:22:05.790 --> 00:22:07.390
Do we to go back?

00:22:07.390 --> 00:22:09.700
Monitoring, doing
necessary cache

00:22:09.700 --> 00:22:12.310
refreshes for us to
be able to do our job.

00:22:12.310 --> 00:22:15.880
And then finally, for
any one of these tasks,

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:18.792
there are three cloud
platform level operations

00:22:18.792 --> 00:22:20.500
that we have to go
ahead and orchestrate,

00:22:20.500 --> 00:22:24.010
so dealing with load balancers,
association, disassociations,

00:22:24.010 --> 00:22:26.410
target pools, and
removing instances.

00:22:26.410 --> 00:22:29.260
And for every single one
of these minute actions--

00:22:29.260 --> 00:22:31.060
if something goes wrong--

00:22:31.060 --> 00:22:34.550
to be able to go out
and auto remediate.

00:22:34.550 --> 00:22:37.760
So, this is a lot that you
would have to actually go and do

00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:40.790
to say, we have velocity
and we have confidence.

00:22:40.790 --> 00:22:43.670
What allows you to go
fast is confidence.

00:22:43.670 --> 00:22:45.500
And this is the
amount of work that

00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:49.220
is done in Spinnaker to go ahead
and promote that confidence.

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:52.910
And you shouldn't have to
build all of this yourself.

00:22:52.910 --> 00:22:57.714
You should go and get behind
an open source project,

00:22:57.714 --> 00:22:59.630
like Spinnaker, which I
will go ahead and show

00:22:59.630 --> 00:23:02.102
you a little more later.

00:23:02.102 --> 00:23:03.810
And so, for automation,
let's go and hear

00:23:03.810 --> 00:23:05.435
what Waze does in a
little more detail.

00:23:08.842 --> 00:23:10.300
TOM FEINER: By the
way, Waze really

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:11.950
didn't want to go
and build this stuff.

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:14.590
We were really happy
Spinnaker existed.

00:23:14.590 --> 00:23:18.790
And we've actually been users
of Asgard for a few years back.

00:23:18.790 --> 00:23:22.660
And we seamlessly transitioned
into Spinnaker from Asgard.

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:23.830
So it's been great.

00:23:23.830 --> 00:23:25.960
And thanks for the
Netflix guys for doing

00:23:25.960 --> 00:23:28.660
this, for open-sourcing.

00:23:28.660 --> 00:23:32.710
So once deployment
pipelines are in place,

00:23:32.710 --> 00:23:36.550
some interesting things
can start to happen.

00:23:36.550 --> 00:23:39.660
We basically can start using
some fail-safe patterns

00:23:39.660 --> 00:23:43.010
to limit the blast
radius of changes.

00:23:43.010 --> 00:23:46.520
For example, at Waze, we
shard everything by geography,

00:23:46.520 --> 00:23:48.100
obviously.

00:23:48.100 --> 00:23:51.130
This allows pipelines
to limit the blast

00:23:51.130 --> 00:23:53.000
radius of each upgrade.

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:56.440
So, for example, we can
upgrade, for a certain service,

00:23:56.440 --> 00:23:59.470
one part of the world
completely using the Red/black

00:23:59.470 --> 00:24:03.140
deployments, then move to a
few more parts of the world,

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:07.130
and eventually, worldwide.

00:24:07.130 --> 00:24:11.790
If you're lucky and your usage
patterns are predictable,

00:24:11.790 --> 00:24:14.850
it's good practice to
use time restriction

00:24:14.850 --> 00:24:16.020
in deployment pipelines.

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:17.850
This allows us to
say, for example,

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:20.400
that a certain pipeline
can run between six and 12

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:24.680
UTC only between
Mondays and Wednesdays.

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:28.570
So this ensures that,
when an upgrade occurs,

00:24:28.570 --> 00:24:31.510
it affects as few
users as possible.

00:24:31.510 --> 00:24:33.670
And if you're really
lucky, you can

00:24:33.670 --> 00:24:36.490
try to find a sweet spot
between the off-peak hours

00:24:36.490 --> 00:24:38.740
and the office
hours, then you have

00:24:38.740 --> 00:24:41.050
staff caffeinated,
and ready to go

00:24:41.050 --> 00:24:43.900
to solve any issue that might
come up during this upgrade.

00:24:49.229 --> 00:24:50.770
STEVEN KIM: The
fourth and final one,

00:24:50.770 --> 00:24:52.720
which I don't think gets
talked about enough,

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:55.000
is operational integration.

00:24:55.000 --> 00:24:57.070
And what operational
integration speaks to

00:24:57.070 --> 00:24:58.840
is the notion that
the process of how

00:24:58.840 --> 00:25:01.200
you go to release
software needs to work

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:04.560
hand-in-hand with how you
operationalize that software.

00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:06.320
And that goes in
both directions,

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:08.540
so two simple examples.

00:25:08.540 --> 00:25:12.520
One is that, when you're
releasing-- build, release,

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:14.410
and promote, and so forth--

00:25:14.410 --> 00:25:18.490
that process is gathering and
generating a lot of information

00:25:18.490 --> 00:25:24.370
that is germane to the scope
where you are doing root cause

00:25:24.370 --> 00:25:26.470
analysis or defect triaging.

00:25:26.470 --> 00:25:28.600
So, for example,
while you're trying

00:25:28.600 --> 00:25:30.940
to go ahead and figure out
why something went out,

00:25:30.940 --> 00:25:32.740
it's useful to go ahead
and understand, oh,

00:25:32.740 --> 00:25:37.600
when we built that software,
what are the commits that

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:40.600
pertain to the change from the
last deployment to the first?

00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:43.210
Here's the blast radius of
the code and config change.

00:25:43.210 --> 00:25:45.400
What kind of validation
tests were run?

00:25:45.400 --> 00:25:47.470
What were the results and
output from that test?

00:25:47.470 --> 00:25:49.053
All this information
that is generated

00:25:49.053 --> 00:25:50.625
at the time of doing
your deployment

00:25:50.625 --> 00:25:52.750
becomes very, very useful
and important when you're

00:25:52.750 --> 00:25:54.400
doing operational things.

00:25:54.400 --> 00:25:56.469
It goes in the other
direction as well.

00:25:56.469 --> 00:25:58.010
So you have systems
that are running.

00:25:58.010 --> 00:26:00.176
And the information that
is gathered as your systems

00:26:00.176 --> 00:26:02.270
run and production
are very, very useful

00:26:02.270 --> 00:26:05.600
as you're doing your
process of doing deploys.

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:10.660
A simple example, as we talked
about, is Canary analysis.

00:26:10.660 --> 00:26:12.980
Canary analysis goes and
looks at how your systems are

00:26:12.980 --> 00:26:16.250
behaving, and it could do it at
point in time or statistically

00:26:16.250 --> 00:26:18.897
across time to go and determine
what is the right validation

00:26:18.897 --> 00:26:21.230
gate to go ahead and say the
system is behaving normally

00:26:21.230 --> 00:26:23.810
as we go to promote from
one environment to another.

00:26:23.810 --> 00:26:25.850
That make sense?

00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:28.850
And finally, release
process inevitably

00:26:28.850 --> 00:26:30.800
crosses multiple teams.

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:34.890
I know there's a lot of good
aspirations out there about

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:38.520
whether it's [? dev-ops ?] or
project methodology like agile

00:26:38.520 --> 00:26:41.040
or whatever it might be, but
your process inevitably has

00:26:41.040 --> 00:26:43.500
a lot of orchestration and
coordination that's needed

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:45.600
across people and teams.

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:47.880
And so what that means
is a Spinnaker pipeline,

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:51.150
for example, can help you
communicate and validate

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:54.870
and record hand-offs that
are necessary between teams

00:26:54.870 --> 00:26:56.190
as you do a release process.

00:26:56.190 --> 00:26:59.310
So to give an example
of that, back to Tom.

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:02.380
TOM FEINER: Thank you.

00:27:02.380 --> 00:27:06.790
We all know real life is messy
and imperfect, especially

00:27:06.790 --> 00:27:09.060
when you're moving fast.

00:27:09.060 --> 00:27:11.670
During a post-mortem
meeting a few months back,

00:27:11.670 --> 00:27:15.210
the team recognized a certain
critical mirco-service

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:18.330
which doesn't have
proper automated testing.

00:27:18.330 --> 00:27:22.060
It will eventually be written,
but that will take time.

00:27:22.060 --> 00:27:24.760
In the meantime,
we decided that we

00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:28.260
want a person, someone
from QA, to run

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:32.440
a sanity after each and every
deployment to production.

00:27:32.440 --> 00:27:33.760
Here's the problem.

00:27:33.760 --> 00:27:36.160
Deciding to do
this is one thing,

00:27:36.160 --> 00:27:39.080
but actually doing this
in a repeatable manner

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:41.950
on every single deployed
production, is easier

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:43.450
said than done.

00:27:43.450 --> 00:27:46.720
People can simply forget,
tasks get shift around.

00:27:46.720 --> 00:27:49.000
Before you know it,
recency bias kicks in,

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:51.850
and we're back to square one.

00:27:51.850 --> 00:27:54.460
So here's how it looks like.

00:27:54.460 --> 00:27:57.680
What we're going to do here is
that, basically, the developer

00:27:57.680 --> 00:27:59.840
commits a code.

00:27:59.840 --> 00:28:03.710
That code that starts a pipeline
in Spinnaker which initiates

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:05.390
a manual judgment stage.

00:28:05.390 --> 00:28:09.530
That manual judgment stage
sends a notification to QA,

00:28:09.530 --> 00:28:13.400
asking them to approve
this upgrade because we

00:28:13.400 --> 00:28:16.820
need resources from them to
be there to run the sanity

00:28:16.820 --> 00:28:18.830
and make a decision.

00:28:18.830 --> 00:28:21.860
Once they approve-- and they
have a button to either approve

00:28:21.860 --> 00:28:24.460
or not approve, or
they can just wait--

00:28:24.460 --> 00:28:28.900
Spinnaker takes over, runs
a full Red/black deployment.

00:28:28.900 --> 00:28:31.840
And once that's done,
another notification

00:28:31.840 --> 00:28:36.990
is sent to QA, asking
to run the sanity.

00:28:36.990 --> 00:28:40.320
Then they have an option
to either pass the sanity

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:44.050
and finish the automatic
deployment, or roll back.

00:28:44.050 --> 00:28:46.200
And Spinnaker takes
care of all of this.

00:28:46.200 --> 00:28:50.220
Basically, going forward just
means deleting the old version,

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:53.480
and moving backwards just
means enabling the old version

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:56.100
and destroying the new one.

00:28:56.100 --> 00:28:57.750
This has been very
helpful for us.

00:29:04.600 --> 00:29:09.180
STEVEN KIM: So we've talked
about four important ideas.

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:11.310
And I know it's
a lot to take in.

00:29:11.310 --> 00:29:15.360
And we've worked hard to
make this sort of consumable

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:20.010
and easy to adopt and create
a ramp instead of a step.

00:29:20.010 --> 00:29:23.850
So let me walk you through
a simple, basic demo that

00:29:23.850 --> 00:29:28.620
takes source code, and
through Spinnaker, applying

00:29:28.620 --> 00:29:31.020
the key principles that
we talked about, deploys

00:29:31.020 --> 00:29:34.920
and promotes something
out to production.

00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:38.390
Can we have the demo PC, please?

00:29:38.390 --> 00:29:38.940
All right.

00:29:42.540 --> 00:29:44.300
[? Simple ?] go app.

00:29:44.300 --> 00:29:47.870
And really, it just goes and
responds with, hello world,

00:29:47.870 --> 00:29:51.260
and the background
color is yellow.

00:29:51.260 --> 00:29:52.460
So let's make a change--

00:29:56.244 --> 00:29:56.744
purple.

00:30:01.901 --> 00:30:02.900
I made it purple, right?

00:30:02.900 --> 00:30:03.608
TOM FEINER: Yeah.

00:30:07.670 --> 00:30:11.000
STEVEN KIM: So I'm going to go
ahead and initiate my release

00:30:11.000 --> 00:30:14.660
process by pushing to the
release branch and source.

00:30:14.660 --> 00:30:19.184
So what happens here
is, first of all,

00:30:19.184 --> 00:30:21.350
right now we'll see that--
what you're looking at is

00:30:21.350 --> 00:30:22.580
a Spinnaker--

00:30:22.580 --> 00:30:27.420
and we see a staging environment
and a production environment.

00:30:27.420 --> 00:30:31.327
And in the staging environment,
if we go to what's out there,

00:30:31.327 --> 00:30:32.660
it says "hello world" in yellow.

00:30:35.350 --> 00:30:37.400
And also in the
production environment,

00:30:37.400 --> 00:30:41.250
it also says "hello
world" in yellow.

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:44.160
So far, so good.

00:30:44.160 --> 00:30:48.210
And you'll also notice
things like the version

00:30:48.210 --> 00:30:49.954
of the container, the
tag on it, matches.

00:30:49.954 --> 00:30:51.620
Its the same thing
that's been promoted.

00:30:51.620 --> 00:30:53.510
We've gone through this once.

00:30:53.510 --> 00:30:56.210
When I committed that
code, what I have set up

00:30:56.210 --> 00:30:57.860
is a build trigger.

00:30:57.860 --> 00:31:00.667
This is not in
Spinnaker, but you

00:31:00.667 --> 00:31:02.750
can go ahead and build
your containers in any way.

00:31:02.750 --> 00:31:05.990
Docker Hub and Quay both
have build triggers.

00:31:05.990 --> 00:31:09.050
And I set up a
trigger in GCR build

00:31:09.050 --> 00:31:13.870
so that when there is a push to
the release branch, I go ahead

00:31:13.870 --> 00:31:15.580
and trigger a build.

00:31:15.580 --> 00:31:19.270
And I can use the Dockerfile
that's sitting in there,

00:31:19.270 --> 00:31:24.430
but I want to use a feature
called GCR Build Steps which

00:31:24.430 --> 00:31:27.100
allows me to go ahead and
build, among other things,

00:31:27.100 --> 00:31:28.244
lean containers.

00:31:28.244 --> 00:31:30.160
I just want to go ahead
and take a quick segue

00:31:30.160 --> 00:31:33.160
to go ahead and talk
about this a little bit.

00:31:33.160 --> 00:31:36.130
The notion is that instead
of-- a typical Dockerfile

00:31:36.130 --> 00:31:39.040
looks like something like
from this base image,

00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:42.010
pull in this source, go ahead
and build it, and then identify

00:31:42.010 --> 00:31:43.119
an entry point.

00:31:43.119 --> 00:31:45.160
And then that whole thing
happens in a container.

00:31:45.160 --> 00:31:48.730
One of the challenges
there is that everything

00:31:48.730 --> 00:31:53.335
that you needed to build it
stays with you as you go ahead

00:31:53.335 --> 00:31:56.304
and run it-- so the SDK,
dependent packages, all

00:31:56.304 --> 00:31:57.970
these things that you
really don't need.

00:31:57.970 --> 00:32:01.520
It's a general notion that
how you go out and build

00:32:01.520 --> 00:32:05.350
in a validation context
is very different than how

00:32:05.350 --> 00:32:08.440
you might want to build for what
you actually run in production.

00:32:08.440 --> 00:32:10.000
It's not really controversial.

00:32:10.000 --> 00:32:12.670
And so, what you ideally like
to do is do it in two steps.

00:32:12.670 --> 00:32:16.030
One step, I want you to go
ahead and build my binary.

00:32:16.030 --> 00:32:19.827
And then the second step,
containerize just that binary.

00:32:19.827 --> 00:32:21.410
So that's what I've
defined over here.

00:32:21.410 --> 00:32:22.784
If you guys want
to look at more,

00:32:22.784 --> 00:32:26.960
you should go ahead and
Google Container Builder.

00:32:29.640 --> 00:32:32.100
And what you'll
see is I have the

00:32:32.100 --> 00:32:35.100
build that happened just now.

00:32:35.100 --> 00:32:39.320
And if I look at my Container
Registry for that service,

00:32:39.320 --> 00:32:41.300
you'll notice that
it's like four

00:32:41.300 --> 00:32:45.740
megabytes for that
"hello world" container.

00:32:45.740 --> 00:32:48.410
Previously, I had built it
using the standard method,

00:32:48.410 --> 00:32:51.900
and it is 241 megabytes.

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:54.500
So that's like 1 and
1/2 orders of magnitude

00:32:54.500 --> 00:32:56.300
we're talking about there.

00:32:56.300 --> 00:32:59.690
And it's because the Go SDK
is in that container as well

00:32:59.690 --> 00:33:02.450
as all the different
packages that I downloaded.

00:33:02.450 --> 00:33:05.270
OK, so back to our
normally scheduled program.

00:33:07.880 --> 00:33:11.990
So in Spinnaker, I
go ahead and manage

00:33:11.990 --> 00:33:15.200
the process the production
in three pipelines--

00:33:15.200 --> 00:33:20.240
deploy to stage, validate,
and then promote to prod.

00:33:20.240 --> 00:33:25.000
So the first stage, first
pipeline, looks like this.

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:28.090
The configuration is set
such that every time there

00:33:28.090 --> 00:33:33.850
is a change to my Docker
registry, a new tag appears.

00:33:33.850 --> 00:33:37.630
I want you to pull that down
and trigger this pipeline.

00:33:37.630 --> 00:33:41.980
And it really has just
one stage, deploy.

00:33:41.980 --> 00:33:47.150
And deploy-- I'll look at
the deployment details--

00:33:47.150 --> 00:33:52.250
sends it to a stack I designated
as the staging environment.

00:33:52.250 --> 00:33:56.630
It deploys the container
that trigger the pipeline.

00:33:56.630 --> 00:33:59.140
And it uses the
Red/black strategy.

00:33:59.140 --> 00:34:00.890
And what that means
is, once again,

00:34:00.890 --> 00:34:03.290
it goes and brings up a new
[? assisted ?] Kubernetes.

00:34:03.290 --> 00:34:04.954
So it's going to
be a replica set.

00:34:04.954 --> 00:34:06.620
And then once it's
deemed to be healthy,

00:34:06.620 --> 00:34:11.100
it'll cut over the load balancer
and disable the other one.

00:34:11.100 --> 00:34:13.610
And you'll notice that I
checked on the box that

00:34:13.610 --> 00:34:16.409
says "scale down,
replace the server

00:34:16.409 --> 00:34:17.840
groups to zero instances."

00:34:17.840 --> 00:34:19.670
This is a non-production
environment.

00:34:19.670 --> 00:34:21.580
I don't have to cut
back in a hurry.

00:34:21.580 --> 00:34:22.969
Let's save on the resources.

00:34:28.679 --> 00:34:29.950
So that's running right now.

00:34:29.950 --> 00:34:31.699
And you can see, once
again as I described

00:34:31.699 --> 00:34:35.480
before, for that one deploy
stage, it's deploying,

00:34:35.480 --> 00:34:36.679
shrinking the cluster.

00:34:36.679 --> 00:34:38.304
Under each of those,
you can go and see

00:34:38.304 --> 00:34:42.770
the individual tasks that
are being managed to do this.

00:34:42.770 --> 00:34:45.920
So the next pipeline
is called validate.

00:34:45.920 --> 00:34:49.730
And the validate pipeline
is set to trigger

00:34:49.730 --> 00:34:51.560
when the deploy
to stage pipeline

00:34:51.560 --> 00:34:53.929
completes successfully,
the first one that I just

00:34:53.929 --> 00:34:55.870
showed you.

00:34:55.870 --> 00:34:58.320
And it has two simple steps.

00:34:58.320 --> 00:35:01.370
The first one is
a validate stage.

00:35:01.370 --> 00:35:05.690
And this is what we call a
run job stage in Spinnaker.

00:35:05.690 --> 00:35:09.470
And it basically allows you to
point to an arbitrary container

00:35:09.470 --> 00:35:10.480
and run it.

00:35:10.480 --> 00:35:12.740
And whatever the exit status
for that container is,

00:35:12.740 --> 00:35:14.540
it'll go ahead and
proceed accordingly.

00:35:14.540 --> 00:35:18.340
And for me, it's
actually just a simple--

00:35:18.340 --> 00:35:19.640
I think it's a curl command.

00:35:19.640 --> 00:35:22.610
It goes and curls the
endpoint that I specify,

00:35:22.610 --> 00:35:24.620
which is my staging endpoint.

00:35:24.620 --> 00:35:26.270
And if it comes back
with a 200, it'll

00:35:26.270 --> 00:35:29.400
say it's a zero exit code.

00:35:29.400 --> 00:35:31.030
It'll continue.

00:35:31.030 --> 00:35:32.840
And then finally,
at that point, I

00:35:32.840 --> 00:35:37.700
have a manual judgment stage
which notifies somebody.

00:35:37.700 --> 00:35:40.400
And then that person
has to go and say,

00:35:40.400 --> 00:35:42.315
yep, everything looks
good, yes, or no.

00:35:42.315 --> 00:35:43.190
This is, by the way--

00:35:43.190 --> 00:35:44.481
I'll take a moment real quick--

00:35:44.481 --> 00:35:47.070
continuous delivery and
continuous deployment.

00:35:47.070 --> 00:35:49.010
So continuous deployment
is all the way

00:35:49.010 --> 00:35:50.576
to production automated.

00:35:50.576 --> 00:35:51.950
And continuous
delivery typically

00:35:51.950 --> 00:35:54.657
entails somebody being a
gate to go and say, yeah,

00:35:54.657 --> 00:35:55.490
this is ready to go.

00:35:58.940 --> 00:36:02.150
Finally-- actually, let me
see if that came through.

00:36:02.150 --> 00:36:03.380
Oh, yeah, here we go.

00:36:03.380 --> 00:36:09.790
So at this point, you'll notice
that my staging environment--

00:36:09.790 --> 00:36:10.790
please be purple.

00:36:10.790 --> 00:36:11.498
Please be purple.

00:36:11.498 --> 00:36:12.222
It's purple.

00:36:12.222 --> 00:36:12.930
TOM FEINER: Yeah!

00:36:12.930 --> 00:36:14.762
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:14.762 --> 00:36:17.220
STEVEN KIM: And you notice that
the container tags are also

00:36:17.220 --> 00:36:19.770
different, right?

00:36:19.770 --> 00:36:20.490
So it looks good.

00:36:20.490 --> 00:36:22.650
I'm going to go
ahead and promote it.

00:36:22.650 --> 00:36:28.380
So when I hit Continue, my third
and final pipeline will run.

00:36:28.380 --> 00:36:31.330
And here's what that looks like.

00:36:31.330 --> 00:36:33.060
The configuration,
as you might imagine,

00:36:33.060 --> 00:36:34.940
is set so that
this pipeline will

00:36:34.940 --> 00:36:37.790
trigger when the validate
pipeline completes

00:36:37.790 --> 00:36:39.490
successfully.

00:36:39.490 --> 00:36:41.700
And it'll do a couple of things.

00:36:41.700 --> 00:36:44.930
First, I find the image from
the staging environment.

00:36:44.930 --> 00:36:45.880
I know it was good.

00:36:45.880 --> 00:36:47.520
I tested it.

00:36:47.520 --> 00:36:50.360
I want to use that instead
of rebuilding or locating

00:36:50.360 --> 00:36:52.064
that image otherwise.

00:36:52.064 --> 00:36:54.230
And so I can give it
coordinates to go ahead and say

00:36:54.230 --> 00:36:58.880
from the staging environment,
take the image from the newest

00:36:58.880 --> 00:37:02.460
group that's out there
and give me that image.

00:37:02.460 --> 00:37:06.560
I take that image,
and I deploy a Canary.

00:37:06.560 --> 00:37:10.190
And for that deployment, I go
ahead and specify the Canary

00:37:10.190 --> 00:37:11.240
environment.

00:37:11.240 --> 00:37:12.740
I don't use any
strategies because I

00:37:12.740 --> 00:37:14.630
don't want to manipulate
traffic in any way.

00:37:14.630 --> 00:37:16.670
I just want to bring
something up and put it

00:37:16.670 --> 00:37:18.450
into the same load balancer.

00:37:18.450 --> 00:37:21.390
And I put up one instance.

00:37:23.910 --> 00:37:27.000
So now we have the
old production as is,

00:37:27.000 --> 00:37:28.110
at full capacity.

00:37:28.110 --> 00:37:31.800
And then we have one instance
of the new version in a Canary

00:37:31.800 --> 00:37:35.250
environment, both taking traffic
from the same load balancer.

00:37:35.250 --> 00:37:36.630
So what will happen
at this point

00:37:36.630 --> 00:37:42.840
is your users are visiting
and 1/9, or however many ever

00:37:42.840 --> 00:37:45.557
that percentage comes out
to, will get the new version.

00:37:45.557 --> 00:37:46.890
And you'll start gathering data.

00:37:46.890 --> 00:37:49.350
And the idea is to start
gathering telemetry

00:37:49.350 --> 00:37:50.570
around how well that's going.

00:37:54.330 --> 00:37:57.060
If that looks good,
after the Canary is done,

00:37:57.060 --> 00:37:59.190
I want you to go ahead
and notify somebody.

00:37:59.190 --> 00:38:02.670
They'll look at the
Canary data and approve.

00:38:02.670 --> 00:38:05.340
And then at that point, they'll
do a Red/black deployment

00:38:05.340 --> 00:38:07.120
to production.

00:38:07.120 --> 00:38:09.270
So it looks good.

00:38:09.270 --> 00:38:11.610
Let's go ahead and cut over
the production environment

00:38:11.610 --> 00:38:14.010
using Red/black to
the new version, which

00:38:14.010 --> 00:38:15.480
will disable the old one.

00:38:15.480 --> 00:38:18.990
But you'll notice,
or I'll point to,

00:38:18.990 --> 00:38:21.720
that I deployed prod,
I use Red/black,

00:38:21.720 --> 00:38:24.540
but I don't scale down the
replaced server groups.

00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:26.830
So they're sitting
there, not taking traffic

00:38:26.830 --> 00:38:29.310
because Red/black disabled
them, but it's sitting there.

00:38:29.310 --> 00:38:29.919
Why?

00:38:29.919 --> 00:38:32.460
Because if something goes wrong,
we want to able to cut over,

00:38:32.460 --> 00:38:33.780
cut back, very, very quickly.

00:38:33.780 --> 00:38:36.877
And we have ad hoc operations
where you can click a button,

00:38:36.877 --> 00:38:38.960
and then it goes and rolls
back, sends traffic out

00:38:38.960 --> 00:38:42.890
of the old one again, instantly.

00:38:42.890 --> 00:38:46.490
And then we tear
down the Canary.

00:38:46.490 --> 00:38:48.740
And once again, we
use locators that

00:38:48.740 --> 00:38:51.187
say the newest server group
in the Canary environment,

00:38:51.187 --> 00:38:52.395
go ahead and tear that down--

00:38:55.150 --> 00:38:58.120
destroy server group stage.

00:38:58.120 --> 00:39:00.650
And at this point,
Canary's dead.

00:39:00.650 --> 00:39:02.690
We have just old prod, new prod.

00:39:02.690 --> 00:39:05.842
Old prod is disabled.

00:39:05.842 --> 00:39:06.800
Let's not be too hasty.

00:39:06.800 --> 00:39:09.500
Let's force a two-hour
wait because at worst,

00:39:09.500 --> 00:39:12.650
all it's doing is
tying down resources.

00:39:12.650 --> 00:39:17.450
Then destroy that old prod.

00:39:17.450 --> 00:39:21.110
So at this point, if I look
at where my pipelines are,

00:39:21.110 --> 00:39:22.700
my Canary went through.

00:39:22.700 --> 00:39:24.920
It's waiting for
manual judgment.

00:39:24.920 --> 00:39:29.910
And if I go back to my clusters
view, here's my production.

00:39:29.910 --> 00:39:31.150
And here is my Canary.

00:39:33.367 --> 00:39:35.700
So I don't know what to really
expect because I've never

00:39:35.700 --> 00:39:40.470
done this with more than me.

00:39:40.470 --> 00:39:43.460
Everybody, pull out your phones.

00:39:43.460 --> 00:39:45.230
Can we go back to
the slides, please?

00:39:54.120 --> 00:39:55.900
That is the production endpoint.

00:39:55.900 --> 00:40:02.800
So, if you please navigate to
mdservice.spinnaker-test.net,

00:40:02.800 --> 00:40:09.100
what should happen is most of
you should get a yellow page.

00:40:09.100 --> 00:40:11.080
And about one out
of every 10 of you

00:40:11.080 --> 00:40:13.580
should be getting a purple page.

00:40:13.580 --> 00:40:16.752
So once you have it loaded up,
can you guys raise your phones

00:40:16.752 --> 00:40:17.710
and point it toward me?

00:40:21.050 --> 00:40:21.990
TOM FEINER: Oh, yeah.

00:40:21.990 --> 00:40:22.642
It worked!

00:40:22.642 --> 00:40:23.600
STEVEN KIM: Oh, my god.

00:40:23.600 --> 00:40:24.225
TOM FEINER: Oh!

00:40:27.172 --> 00:40:28.380
Well, you can't see it, but--

00:40:28.380 --> 00:40:28.590
STEVEN KIM: All right.

00:40:28.590 --> 00:40:29.020
I'll go ahead and
point that over to--

00:40:29.020 --> 00:40:31.019
TOM FEINER: Some are
purple and some are yellow.

00:40:34.300 --> 00:40:36.739
STEVEN KIM: And so at this
point, it's going to look good.

00:40:36.739 --> 00:40:37.530
Canary looked good.

00:40:37.530 --> 00:40:38.535
It showed up purple.

00:40:42.212 --> 00:40:43.170
I'm going hit Continue.

00:40:43.170 --> 00:40:45.889
And it will proceed on to
go and destroy the old prod.

00:40:45.889 --> 00:40:47.430
Is that pretty
straightforward to you

00:40:47.430 --> 00:40:48.850
guys about how that would work?

00:40:48.850 --> 00:40:51.433
Now a couple of things that are
important that happened there.

00:40:51.433 --> 00:40:52.395
It was all automated.

00:40:52.395 --> 00:40:54.270
There was every opportunity
where we recorded

00:40:54.270 --> 00:40:55.353
every step that was taken.

00:40:55.353 --> 00:40:57.360
It followed a process
that we defined.

00:40:57.360 --> 00:40:59.310
And, really, at the point where
we get comfortable with things

00:40:59.310 --> 00:41:01.101
that can go wrong, we
can start cutting out

00:41:01.101 --> 00:41:03.037
those manual approval stages.

00:41:03.037 --> 00:41:04.620
And we can get much
smarter than this.

00:41:04.620 --> 00:41:06.000
This is a demo scenario.

00:41:06.000 --> 00:41:08.010
If you ask what Waze is
doing with Spinnaker,

00:41:08.010 --> 00:41:09.942
or what Netflix is
doing with Spinnaker,

00:41:09.942 --> 00:41:11.400
or a number of
other organizations,

00:41:11.400 --> 00:41:13.350
they're doing far more
sophisticated things.

00:41:13.350 --> 00:41:15.099
Like, for example,
Waze is using Spinnaker

00:41:15.099 --> 00:41:17.460
to go ahead and do fleet
upgrades of base OS

00:41:17.460 --> 00:41:19.255
based on pipelines that
go ahead and handle

00:41:19.255 --> 00:41:20.130
all of this for them.

00:41:25.290 --> 00:41:30.290
So, as I mentioned, we
open-sourced in 2015.

00:41:30.290 --> 00:41:35.300
And we open-sourced with
GCE and EC2 providers.

00:41:35.300 --> 00:41:39.080
As I said, Spinnaker
is multi-cloud.

00:41:39.080 --> 00:41:41.300
And a Cloud Foundry
provider came out there

00:41:41.300 --> 00:41:42.290
in beta at the time.

00:41:42.290 --> 00:41:46.100
And we had Red/black strategy
and some basic support.

00:41:46.100 --> 00:41:49.490
In 2016, we were headed
down development,

00:41:49.490 --> 00:41:51.620
and we introduced the
Kubernertes provider.

00:41:51.620 --> 00:41:53.480
The Azure provider
joined in beta.

00:41:53.480 --> 00:41:54.830
Microsoft joined.

00:41:54.830 --> 00:41:57.559
OpenStack provider--
and, really, those people

00:41:57.559 --> 00:41:59.600
from the community who
are building these things,

00:41:59.600 --> 00:42:01.490
some of them are here
in the room right now.

00:42:01.490 --> 00:42:02.960
So thank you very much.

00:42:02.960 --> 00:42:05.030
We added authentication
and authorization support

00:42:05.030 --> 00:42:09.770
through OAuth 2, Google Groups,
GitHub team, SAML, [INAUDIBLE].

00:42:09.770 --> 00:42:13.160
We added much more robust
support for load balancers,

00:42:13.160 --> 00:42:17.480
layer 3 and 4, layer 7
HTP, internal SSL support.

00:42:17.480 --> 00:42:18.980
We integrated a
Stackdriver logging.

00:42:18.980 --> 00:42:21.313
So we're trying to make this
operationally ready for you

00:42:21.313 --> 00:42:23.300
guys to go ahead and take on.

00:42:23.300 --> 00:42:25.940
And support for things like
autoscaling and autohealing.

00:42:25.940 --> 00:42:27.481
And also we're
beginning to reach out

00:42:27.481 --> 00:42:31.280
into other popular systems
in the ecosystem like Consul

00:42:31.280 --> 00:42:37.130
for doing things like
discovery and health readiness.

00:42:37.130 --> 00:42:40.010
And in 2017, what
we're working on

00:42:40.010 --> 00:42:42.800
is we're going to be adding
an App Engine provider.

00:42:42.800 --> 00:42:44.600
And so that's actually
pretty much done.

00:42:44.600 --> 00:42:46.349
We're ready to start
taking alpha testers.

00:42:46.349 --> 00:42:49.970
So I'll tell you how to go
and reach out to us later.

00:42:49.970 --> 00:42:54.020
We're beginning on formal
support for Canary Strategy.

00:42:54.020 --> 00:42:55.929
That's available
in the OSS realm.

00:42:55.929 --> 00:42:58.220
So Google and Netflix are
working very closely on this.

00:42:58.220 --> 00:43:00.761
And we expect to be at a pretty
good point in the latter half

00:43:00.761 --> 00:43:02.415
of this year with that.

00:43:02.415 --> 00:43:04.790
We're going to be introducing
rolling Red/black strategy.

00:43:04.790 --> 00:43:08.360
That's also at a very
good place already.

00:43:08.360 --> 00:43:10.580
Another major effort
is declarative CD.

00:43:10.580 --> 00:43:14.119
And you can look at
it as config as code,

00:43:14.119 --> 00:43:15.410
but it's really more than that.

00:43:15.410 --> 00:43:18.380
It's more of a holistic
look at when we go and say,

00:43:18.380 --> 00:43:22.730
here's an application, so far we
think about binary and config.

00:43:22.730 --> 00:43:25.880
But also we want to go to make
that bundle be defined as,

00:43:25.880 --> 00:43:29.210
and here's how you should deploy
me, and manage me, upgrade me,

00:43:29.210 --> 00:43:30.061
and so forth.

00:43:30.061 --> 00:43:32.060
So what that means is
that as you have Spinnaker

00:43:32.060 --> 00:43:34.640
in your organization, you can
have application development

00:43:34.640 --> 00:43:37.490
teams who should be
self-sufficient, define all

00:43:37.490 --> 00:43:40.260
their artifacts as code,
check it into a repository,

00:43:40.260 --> 00:43:44.510
and have Spinnaker enact
that on their behalf.

00:43:44.510 --> 00:43:47.570
Spinnaker is a complex system--

00:43:47.570 --> 00:43:49.710
many micro services,
lots of configuration,

00:43:49.710 --> 00:43:53.030
and we recognize that this is
a high friction to adoption.

00:43:53.030 --> 00:43:57.320
So in about a month, we'll
be introducing formally

00:43:57.320 --> 00:43:58.370
versioned releases.

00:43:58.370 --> 00:44:01.506
And so we will run the
full, synthetic transaction

00:44:01.506 --> 00:44:03.380
integration test for
you to go ahead and say,

00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:05.810
[? here ?] is the combination
of different services

00:44:05.810 --> 00:44:08.940
that we have validated, and
work for these providers.

00:44:08.940 --> 00:44:12.020
So we can look and know
what you're getting.

00:44:12.020 --> 00:44:16.100
And also, we'll be done with
a Spinnaker configuration

00:44:16.100 --> 00:44:21.650
management tool that allows you
to install, upgrade, configure,

00:44:21.650 --> 00:44:24.110
and validate your Spinnaker
environment as well.

00:44:24.110 --> 00:44:27.380
There's a lot of documentation
and doubt there right now

00:44:27.380 --> 00:44:30.050
currently, but we
think that this tool

00:44:30.050 --> 00:44:32.810
is going to be a necessity for
people to really adopt and be

00:44:32.810 --> 00:44:34.827
successful with this.

00:44:34.827 --> 00:44:36.410
And finally, we're
going to be filling

00:44:36.410 --> 00:44:39.870
in operational monitoring for
the Spinnaker instance itself,

00:44:39.870 --> 00:44:41.700
so that if what's
deploying your software

00:44:41.700 --> 00:44:44.500
might have performance issues,
or something goes down,

00:44:44.500 --> 00:44:46.850
that somebody can
be notified of that.

00:44:46.850 --> 00:44:48.870
And much more.

00:44:51.845 --> 00:44:53.970
Really, there's actually
some really exciting stuff

00:44:53.970 --> 00:44:54.870
that you'll hear from us.

00:44:54.870 --> 00:44:55.828
It's not little things.

00:44:55.828 --> 00:44:58.050
It's pretty cool things.

00:44:58.050 --> 00:45:02.850
So spinnaker.io is our website.

00:45:02.850 --> 00:45:06.480
And we develop out in the open,
so if you go to the Spinnaker

00:45:06.480 --> 00:45:10.470
GitHub organization, you can
see everything that we're doing.

00:45:10.470 --> 00:45:12.930
The best way to get engaged
is our Slack channel.

00:45:12.930 --> 00:45:16.350
We are a community
that's 2,000 strong--

00:45:16.350 --> 00:45:17.580
heavy participation.

00:45:17.580 --> 00:45:19.410
People are helping
each other out.

00:45:19.410 --> 00:45:22.950
It's a great place to come, get
introduced into the community,

00:45:22.950 --> 00:45:24.780
ask for any level of help.

00:45:24.780 --> 00:45:28.380
People are extremely friendly
and helpful over there.

00:45:28.380 --> 00:45:31.069
And also, just I'll
mention as a shortcut,

00:45:31.069 --> 00:45:33.360
if you want to quickly get
an instance of Spinnaker out

00:45:33.360 --> 00:45:35.490
for yourself to
play around today,

00:45:35.490 --> 00:45:40.890
go to cloud.google.com/launcher,
search for Spinnaker,

00:45:40.890 --> 00:45:43.510
and you can, in one click, get
an instance of Spinnaker up

00:45:43.510 --> 00:45:45.420
that you can go and
start working with.

00:45:45.420 --> 00:45:47.610
There are also a
set of code labs

00:45:47.610 --> 00:45:50.890
on spinnaker.io that you
can go ahead and look at.

00:45:50.890 --> 00:45:52.840
That's how you can go
ahead and get started.

00:45:52.840 --> 00:45:55.290
[MUSIC PLAYING]

